added_lemmas.proof_tex:
Let \(\mathfrak T_\epsilon\) be the set of Markov kernels from \(\mathcal O^n\) to \([-2,2]\) that are pure \(\epsilon\)-differentially private under replacement adjacency \(\sim_1\).  This is the estimator set in both \(\mathrm{def:private-risk}\) and \(\mathrm{def:kennedy-private-risk}\).  By \(\mathrm{lem:exact-uniform-subclass}\), for every \(T\in\mathfrak T_\epsilon\),
\[
\sup_{P\in\mathcal P_K}
\mathbb E_{P^{\otimes n},T}|T(D_n)-\theta(P)|
\ge
\sup_{P\in\mathcal M_H}
\mathbb E_{P^{\otimes n},T}|T(D_n)-\theta(P)|.
\]
Taking the infimum over the same set \(\mathfrak T_\epsilon\) preserves the inequality and gives
\[
R_{n,\epsilon}^{\mathrm{DP},K}\ge R_{n,\epsilon}^{\mathrm{DP}}. \tag{1}
\]
Define
\[
r_{\mathrm{NP}}
=n^{-1/(2+d/\gamma)}\vee
n^{-2/(2+d/\gamma+d/q)}.
\]
By \(\mathrm{lem:nonprivate-cate-rate-floor}\), there is \(c_{\mathrm{NP}}>0\), depending only on the displayed model parameters, for which
\[
R_n^{\mathrm{NP}}\ge c_{\mathrm{NP}}r_{\mathrm{NP}}. \tag{2}
\]
By \(\mathrm{thm:full-class-lower-frontier}\), the privacy-regime condition \(n\epsilon\ge1\) implies
\[
R_{n,\epsilon}^{\mathrm{DP}}
\ge c_{\mathrm{lower}}
\left(R_n^{\mathrm{NP}}\vee\rho_G\vee\rho_F\right). \tag{3}
\]
Combining (1)--(3) yields
\[
\begin{split}
R_{n,\epsilon}^{\mathrm{DP},K}
&\ge c_{\mathrm{lower}}
\left(c_{\mathrm{NP}}r_{\mathrm{NP}}\vee\rho_G\vee\rho_F\right)\\
&\ge c_{\mathrm{lower}}\min\{c_{\mathrm{NP}},1\}
\left(r_{\mathrm{NP}}\vee\rho_G\vee\rho_F\right).
\end{split}
\]
Taking \(c_K=c_{\mathrm{lower}}\min\{c_{\mathrm{NP}},1\}>0\) proves the claim.  Every branch is transferred by the same inclusion argument, and no attainability condition from Kennedy et al.'s Theorem~2 is used.

proposed.construction:
The pure-private minimax absolute risk over \(\mathcal P_K\) is
\[
R_{n,\epsilon}^{\mathrm{DP},K}
=\inf_{T_{\mathrm{DP}}:\,\epsilon\text{-DP under }\sim_1}
\sup_{P\in\mathcal P_K}
\mathbb E_{P^{\otimes n},T_{\mathrm{DP}}}
\left|T_{\mathrm{DP}}(D_n)-\theta(P)\right|,
\]
where every kernel has domain \(\mathcal O^n\), output space \([-2,2]\), and replacement adjacency \(\sim_1\).  Thus \(R_{n,\epsilon}^{\mathrm{DP},K}\in[0,2]\); the upper endpoint follows from the admissible constant-zero kernel and \(\theta(P)\in[-2,2]\).
